1
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:19,766
Every how-to video plays at the same speed, whether you keep up, or not.

2
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:27,206
Falls are the second-leading cause of unintentional injury death worldwide.

3
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:34,977
In one survey, three in four people didn't finish their home physical therapy exercises.

4
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,798
And most cardiac arrests outside hospital happen at home,

5
00:00:39,798 --> 00:00:42,464
where nobody is checking your form.

6
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,436
At least nine in ten deaf children are born to hearing parents.

7
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,650
And a video can't see whether your hands are right.

8
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,008
InStep is the video that waits for you.

9
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,265
Prop up your phone or laptop. It becomes the camera: an external camera,

10
00:01:06,265 --> 00:01:07,311
not the TV.

11
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:14,656
Pose tracking runs in its browser. Only body points travel to the TV: numbers,

12
00:01:14,656 --> 00:01:15,876
never video.

13
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:22,542
This is Fall Prevention, on the Vega Virtual Device. At each key moment,

14
00:01:22,542 --> 00:01:27,723
the lesson pauses, and continues only when your pose is right.

15
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:34,700
TV: Up, and down.

16
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:41,607
It counts your reps and times your balance. If you're stuck,

17
00:01:41,607 --> 00:01:43,861
it offers one short, kind tip.

18
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:56,984
Safety comes first. No balance work until a chair is beside you.

19
00:01:56,984 --> 00:02:01,345
And if the camera drops, the lesson stops, and waits.

20
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:07,037
Sign Language for Families teaches hand shapes, letter by letter.

21
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:11,200
TV: Letter A. A fist, thumb resting at the side.

22
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:16,675
The hands are illustrated, and not yet reviewed by Deaf signers.

23
00:02:16,675 --> 00:02:19,063
Learn with Deaf teachers, too.

24
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:23,840
For kids, the lesson is a story.

25
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:29,900
TV: Look at the tall trees. Stretch up tall like a tree!

26
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:34,945
The forest waits until they're a tree, a mouse, and a flamingo.

27
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,400
TV: Here’s a flamingo. Can you stand on one foot?

28
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:40,850
TV: Amazing balancing!

29
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:47,033
The same engine runs hand rehab, and CPR rhythm: practice only,

30
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:48,793
not certification.

31
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:53,804
And two people can practise together, each with their own phone.

32
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:00,109
A physiotherapist or teacher records a lesson once, on a phone.

33
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:07,008
InStep finds the still moments, suggests the steps, and publishes it to the TV.

34
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:12,981
Every lesson is an open file, called .istep: a video,

35
00:03:12,981 --> 00:03:15,604
plus the poses it waits for.

36
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,949
The phone sends body points over a WebSocket.

37
00:03:22,949 --> 00:03:26,263
A small server relays them to the TV.

38
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:32,826
One pure TypeScript gate engine, shared by the phone, the server and the TV,

39
00:03:32,826 --> 00:03:34,911
decides when to move on.

40
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,633
And the gated video player is open source, for any Vega app.

41
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:46,666
No learner video leaves the phone. The voices are synthetic,

42
00:03:46,666 --> 00:03:52,250
made on this machine. And the AWS coach is built, but switched off.

43
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,830
InStep. The video that waits for you.

